{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>From before the aged you shall rise, etc.</b> — Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 32b): ״מִפְּנֵי שֵׂיבָה״ — this is one who is great in years; ״תָּקוּם וְהָדַרְתָּ״ — standing that contains embellishment. ״תָּקוּם וְהָדַרְתָּ פְּנֵי זָקֵן״ — this is one who has acquired wisdom. It is difficult: if so, He should have said ״מִפְּנֵי שֵׂיבָה וּמִפְּנֵי זָקֵן תָּקוּם וְהָדַרְתָּ״. Perhaps the intention is to draw the embellishment to this zaken who has acquired wisdom, and the standing to the sevah, which is one advanced in years. And the intention in this is that there must be a greater measure of honor for the wise person than for one great in years. And even though the Sages of blessed memory expounded standing and embellishment for this one and standing and embellishment for that one, with all this, from the fact that the verse was wise to speak in this order — to draw the embellishment to the zaken — it informed us that the embellishment for the zaken is greater than the embellishment for one great in years. From this point onward we find ourselves saying that one must honor the wise person more than one who is aged." "<b>Further</b>, it alludes in accordance with what they said, of blessed memory, in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 65:9), that Abraham our father asked for old age so that the father would be distinguished from the son, etc., as it is written (Genesis 24:1) ״וְאַבְרָהָם זָקֵן״, and this is His saying <b>from before sevah you shall rise</b> — and through this you embellish the face of the zaken, who is Abraham, as it is written ״וְאַבְרָהָם זָקֵן״, for through him this matter came about." "<b>Further</b>, it alludes in accordance with what they said, of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah 12:6), that the wicked are bent of stature, and HaShem commands a person to arouse himself to rectify his deeds to return to HaShem — and this is his standing — and to awaken to rectify his deeds mipnei sevah, meaning his old age, so that it not be lost from him, in accordance with what they said of blessed memory (Yevamot 50a): if he merited, they complete his years for him; if he did not merit, they diminish for him, and he will not reach the days of our years, seventy years. Also it alludes that evil days not come upon him at the time of his old age, which are afflictions harder for a person than any afflictions, as they said of blessed memory (Kohelet Rabbah 12:1), for they are days when a person's powers and efforts are nullified and he requires divine providence." "<b>Further</b>, it alludes: <b>and you shall embellish the face of a zaken</b> — meaning the old age of a person himself, in accordance with what they said (Sukkah 53a): Happy is our youth that did not shame our old age. Also it alludes to what they said of blessed memory (Chagigah 14b), that at the hour when the righteous person departs from this world, they say to Abraham our father: Happy are you, Abraham our father, that this righteous one came forth from your loins; but when the wicked person departs, not only do they not say thus before him, but he is pained. Therefore HaShem arouses a person and says to him <b>and you shall embellish the face of a zaken</b> — this is Abraham, who is embellished when they say before him ״אַשְׁרֶיךָ״ etc. And He added to frighten him with matters of the World to Come, and said <b>and you shall fear from your Elohim</b> — this is fear of the punishment of the Day of Judgment; <b>I am YHWH</b> — this is the payment of good reward of the World to Come. Or it may intend to awaken fear and love: fear — ״וְיָרֵאתָ״ etc.; love — ״אֲנִי ה׳״, for this name is the source of coveted love in the heart of every discerning person."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:47.740059298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}